  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:fuNp6W4DUWs",
    "purpose": "Part two of a community-voted t-shirt roundup. Michael tries on the t-shirt brands most often recommended in comments on his previous t-shirt video, and reports vote share, fabric details, fit, shrinkage after washing and a softness score for each.",
    "video_type": "ranking",
    "thesis": "The internet's most-recommended t-shirts, from $10 basics to $200 luxury tees, are tried on and measured. The most-voted brand, Bronson, gets a closer look because of how it markets its shirts as loopwheel.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The shirts were chosen by tallying brands mentioned in comments on the previous t-shirt video. Vote shares are given for each: Front General Store 3.5%, Samurai 10.34%, Sunspel 13.68%, Warehouse 20.68% and Bronson 25.8%.",
      "Each shirt gets width and length shrink percentages and a softness score out of 10.",
      "The video explains yarn types: open-end, ring-spun and compact.",
      "Merino and alpaca blend tees (Paka, Wool&Prince) are discussed: why animal fibers weren't historically used for tees, and how nylon-wrapped merino changes that.",
      "Samurai's loopwheel claims are covered: half a meter of fabric per hour, and cotton conditioned in a controlled environment.",
      "Loop & Weft is an off-list personal pick, a very slubby tee bought from Redcast Heritage.",
      "The Sunspel Sea Island cotton tee costs $200. Its shrink numbers are high because it was accidentally machine dried; it should be washed like a fine sweater.",
      "The video looks at whether Bronson's 'loopwheel' label is accurate, and at a possibly related brand ('older best') that uses Tompkins knitting machines, which are confirmed not to be loopwheel."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
